{
  "concept": "An element's group position predicts its most common ionic charge for main-group elements (cations in Groups 1–2, anions in Groups 15–17), while transition metals show variable charges not predicted by group number.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "periodic table grid: 7 periods (rows labeled 1–7 at left) and 18 groups (columns labeled 1, 2, 3–12, 13–18 at top)",
    "title 'Periodic Table of the Elements'",
    "axis labels: 'Period' (vertical, left) and 'Group' (top-left)",
    "Group 1 cells labeled Li+, Na+, K+, Rb+, Cs+, Fr+ (purple shading); H cell empty",
    "Group 2 cells labeled Be2+, Mg2+, Ca2+, Sr2+, Ba2+, Ra2+ (green shading)",
    "Group 13 cells with Al3+ labeled (others empty)",
    "Group 14 cell labeled C4−",
    "Group 15 cells labeled N3−, P3−, As3− (pink shading)",
    "Group 16 cells labeled O2−, S2−, Se2−, Te2− (pink shading)",
    "Group 17 cells labeled F−, Cl−, Br−, I−, At− (light blue-lavender shading)",
    "Group 18 cells labeled He, Ne, Ar, Kr, Xe, Rn with no charge (blue shading)",
    "transition-metal block (tan shading) with variable-charge labels: Cr3+/Cr6+, Mn2+, Fe2+/Fe3+, Co2+, Ni2+, Cu+/Cu2+, Zn2+, Ag+, Cd2+, Pt2+, Au+/Au3+, Hg2_2+/Hg2+",
    "red rectangle highlighting the lanthanide/actinide placeholder cells in periods 6–7 of group 3",
    "red arrow from the placeholder to the detached f-block rows",
    "two detached rows below the table: lanthanides (light green, marked *) and actinides (pink-purple, marked **)",
    "legend/annotation panel showing selected element's charge, electron configuration, and group rule"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "elementClick",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Each element's ionic charge follows from its electron configuration — main-group elements gain or lose electrons to reach the nearest noble-gas configuration, which is why charge tracks group position"
    },
    {
      "id": "groupSweep",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Group number",
      "range": [1, 18, 1],
      "default": 1,
      "teaches": "Sweeping group by group reveals the charge pattern: +1, +2 across the left, unpredictable variable charges in the d-block, then −3, −2, −1 approaching the noble gases, which form no ions at all"
    },
    {
      "id": "colorScheme",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Color scheme",
      "options": ["Element type", "Charge value"],
      "default": "Element type",
      "teaches": "Coloring by charge value instead of element type makes the periodic trend directly visible — charge magnitude rises toward both edges of the table and falls to zero at the noble gases"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Alkali and alkaline earth metals",
      "narration": "Sweep the slider through Groups 1 and 2: every element loses electrons equal to its group number, forming +1 and +2 cations to reach the previous noble-gas configuration.",
      "state": { "groupSweep": 1, "colorScheme": "Element type" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Transition metals break the rule",
      "narration": "Move into Groups 3–12: charges become variable (Fe2+/Fe3+, Cu+/Cu2+) because d-electrons are lost in differing numbers — group position no longer reliably predicts the ion.",
      "state": { "groupSweep": 8, "colorScheme": "Element type" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Nonmetal anions and noble gases",
      "narration": "Sweep Groups 15–18: nonmetals gain electrons to reach the next noble gas (N3−, O2−, F−), with the charge equal to how many groups they lie left of Group 18 — and the noble gases themselves form no ions.",
      "state": { "groupSweep": 16, "colorScheme": "Element type" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Recolor by charge",
      "narration": "Switch to charge-value coloring: the whole trend appears at a glance — cations on the left, anions on the right, a variable middle, and a zero-charge noble-gas column.",
      "state": { "groupSweep": 18, "colorScheme": "Charge value" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "For main-group elements the ionic charge is just the electron-count shortcut to the nearest noble gas (+group number on the left, −(18−group) on the right); transition metals are the exception where this rule fails.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Standard 18-column periodic table layout with the gap between Groups 2 and 13 for periods 2–3, and the d-block spanning Groups 3–12 for periods 4–7. Detached lanthanide (light green) and actinide (pink-purple) rows sit below, connected by the red outlined placeholder cells in Group 3 with a curved red arrow and */** markers. Default coloring matches the figure: purple Group 1, green Group 2, tan d-block, pink Groups 15–16, lavender Group 17, blue Group 18. Charge labels appear inside each cell as bold superscripted symbols (e.g. Ca2+, O2−); transition-metal cells with two charges stack both labels. Group numbers in blue above each column, period numbers in purple at left. Charge-value color mode should use a diverging scale (warm for cations, cool for anions, neutral gray for zero/variable). Clicking a cell draws a highlight outline and opens a side panel with the ion, its electron configuration (e.g. Na+ → [Ne]), and the applicable rule."
}

